+### Agent Manual — how agents learn Agent Farm {#agent-manual}
+
+Every function of Agent Farm is a bridge op (parity), so an agent can do anything a person can. The **Agent Manual** is what makes an agent know *which* op, *when* and *how*, without the source and without the user's own rules.
+
+- **What it is:** `manual/` in the extension — a **start card** (`card.md`, ~300 tokens), a **map** (`map.md`), an **area guide** per subsystem (`areas/*.md`), **playbooks** that turn a user's request into ops step by step (`playbooks/*.md`), and `ops.meta.json`, which gives every op an area, an effect (`read · write · run · destructive · ui`), a one-line summary and the cues *asks* (a confirmation dialog) and *plans first*. On every start it is compiled against the live registry, so an op the manual has not described yet still gets a contract page.
+- **How an agent reaches it:** `farm.guide` (map, or `id=<area|playbook|op>`), `farm.find q="<request>"` (ranked playbooks, guides and ops), `farm.ops op=<name>` (one exact contract), `farm.brief` (the caller's live situation: session, agent, project, account, what waits for it, fleet state, the user's language). af.js answers the first three **locally** from `manual.json` staged beside it: no round trip, no notification, and they work while VS Code is closed.
+- **One address on every machine:** `~/.agentfarm/af.js` is a stable launcher written at every start (it forwards to the staged af.js, whatever VS Code flavour or OS). `node "$HOME/.agentfarm/af.js" <op>` works in bash, zsh and PowerShell alike. `AGENT_FARM_HOME` moves it.
+- **Every session is told:** Claude Code and Codex sessions receive the start card through their SessionStart hook (any session on the machine while the hooks are installed); Gemini, Antigravity and OpenCode runs Agent Farm starts carry it in front of their first prompt. Setting `agentFarm.agentManual.startCard` = `card` (default) · `line` · `off`.
+- **Sessions Agent Farm does not start:** `manual.install` (plans first; `confirm=true` writes) installs an `agent-farm` skill for Claude Code (OpenCode reads the same folder) and Codex; `manual.remove` takes it out. Installed skills are refreshed at every start; a skill of the same name Agent Farm did not write is never touched.
+- **Errors that teach:** an unknown op is answered with the closest op names and the search that finds the rest.
+- **It cannot drift:** `test/test_manual_content.js` fails the build when an op has no metadata, when a guide or playbook names an op or an argument the contract does not have, or when a page outgrows its token budget; `test/test_manual_query.js` replays `manual/intents.eval.json` (real requests) and requires the right answer in the top three. `node tools/manual_meta.js` drafts the entry for a new op.
+- **It learns where it fails:** unknown ops, weak searches and missing guides are logged locally (`manual.gaps`), so the manual can be improved where agents actually got lost.
+
 ### Bridge Guard and Search Interceptor (bridgeGuard)
 
 The Agent Farm ecosystem enforces architectural rules mechanically at the hook level rather than relying on passive written instructions (`src/core/bridgeGuard.ts`).
