diff --git a/manual/playbooks/react-to-events.md b/manual/playbooks/react-to-events.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4c23812
--- /dev/null
+++ b/manual/playbooks/react-to-events.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+---
+id: react-to-events
+title: React to a file, folder, agent event or webhook (trigger)
+area: automation
+summary: Install a trigger that starts a task, the dispatcher or a workflow when a watched path changes, an agent hook fires, or a webhook is called.
+intents:
+  - when a file lands in this folder have the agent process it
+  - watch the inbox folder
+  - when the designer finishes, start the reviewer
+  - give me a webhook url to start this workflow from n8n
+  - run the workflow whenever this file changes
+keywords: [trigger, watch, folder, file, when, webhook, hook, event, n8n, zapier, on change]
+ops: [agents.list, workflows.list, triggers.add, triggers.list, triggers.fire, triggers.edit, reports.triggers]
+effect: write
+---
+## Choose the type
+- `type=watch` with `watch=<existing path>`: a file or folder changes. Prompts can use `{file}` (what changed) and `{watch}`.
+- `type=hook` with `hookEvent=stop|start|prompt|toolStart|toolEnd|permission|notify|subagent|compact|end` and `hookAgent=<agent>`: an agent session event.
+- `type=webhook`: Agent Farm returns a local POST URL; the body is `{payload}`.
+
+## Steps
+1. `agents.list` (and `workflows.list` for a workflow action).
+2. Install (file payload):
+```json
+{ "op": "triggers.add", "args": { "title": "Process inbox", "type": "watch", "watch": "<folder>", "action": "task", "agent": "<agent>", "prompt": "A new file arrived: {file}. Process it as follows: ...", "debounceSec": 30, "fireIf": { "kind": "folderHasFiles", "path": "<folder>", "minCount": 1, "except": ["_LOG_.md"] } } }
+```
+   For a workflow: `"action": "workflow", "workflow": "<title>"`, optionally `"inputs": {"<input>": "{file}"}`. For routing to the best agent: `"action": "dispatch"`.
+3. Read back: `triggers.list`. Test only with the user's consent (it starts a run): `triggers.fire id=<title> file=<path>`.
+
+## Pitfalls
+- A folder watch fires on any change: always add `fireIf` and a `debounceSec`, and prefer a daily schedule with a precondition when latency does not matter.
+- Reliability over time: `reports.triggers`.
diff --git a/manual/playbooks/recurring-task.md b/manual/playbooks/recurring-task.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..01143e8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/manual/playbooks/recurring-task.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+---
+id: recurring-task
+title: Run something on a schedule (daily, weekly, once)
+area: automation
+summary: Install a schedule that queues an agent run at a set local time, with a precondition so it only wakes when there is work.
+intents:
+  - every day at 9 have the assistant prepare a briefing
+  - every monday morning run the weekly report
+  - create a recurring task that runs daily at this time
+  - remind me tomorrow at 10
+  - schedule this job once on friday at 18:00
+  - run this every weekday
+keywords: [schedule, daily, weekly, once, recurring, every, cron, at, morning, night, weekday, remind]
+ops: [agents.list, schedules.add, schedules.list, schedules.runNow, schedules.forecast, schedules.edit, automations.status]
+effect: write
+---
+## Ask only if missing
+Agent, time, and what the run should do. Days for weekly. Whether it should skip when there is nothing to do (suggest a `fireIf`).
+
+## Map the request
+- "every day at 09:30" → `kind=daily hour=9 minute=30`.
+- "every Monday at 08:00" → `kind=weekly dow=1 hour=8 minute=0` (0 Sunday ... 6 Saturday).
+- "every weekday" → five weekly schedules, `dow` 1 to 5, distinct titles (for example "Briefing (Mon)").
+- "once on 2026-11-20 at 18:00" → `kind=once onceAt="2026-11-20 18:00"`.
+- "every hour" is not a schedule kind: propose a watch trigger with a precondition (playbook react-to-events) or a few fixed times.
+Times are the machine's local time.
+
+## Steps
+1. `agents.list`; make sure the title is new (`schedules.list`).
+2. Install (file payload):
+```json
+{ "op": "schedules.add", "args": { "agent": "<agent>", "title": "Morning briefing", "prompt": "<what to do each time; point at the agent's duty if one exists>", "kind": "daily", "hour": 9, "minute": 0, "missed": "skip", "fireIf": { "kind": "folderHasFiles", "path": "<inbox folder>", "minCount": 1 } } }
+```
+   Omit `fireIf` only when the run must happen every time. Add run dimensions (`engine`, `model`, `permissionMode`, `project`) as the user wants; an unattended run cannot ask for permissions.
+3. Read back: `schedules.list` (it shows the dimensions it will fire with) and `schedules.forecast days=7`.
+4. Optional test, only if the user agrees to pay for one run now: `schedules.runNow id=<title>`.
+
+## Tell the user
+Title, agent, when it fires next, what it skips on, and how to change it (`schedules.edit`) or pause it (`schedules.toggle`).
+
+## Pitfalls
+- The automation gate (`automations.status`) can hold it: master switch off, paused, or quiet hours (deferred, not dropped).
+- A schedule runs a task prompt; to run a workflow on a timer, see playbook react-to-events or have the scheduled agent run it.
+- Do not duplicate long procedures in the prompt; point at the agent's duty or instructions.
diff --git a/manual/playbooks/restart-or-update.md b/manual/playbooks/restart-or-update.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..767b844
--- /dev/null
+++ b/manual/playbooks/restart-or-update.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+id: restart-or-update
+title: Restart Agent Farm or load a newly installed build
+area: basics
+summary: Check what is running, then restart the extension host or reload the window without losing other people's work.
+intents:
+  - restart agent farm
+  - load the new version
+  - the extension seems stuck, restart it
+keywords: [restart, reload, update, new version, install, build, vsix, stuck extension]
+ops: [farm.health, farm.restartExtensionHost, farm.reloadWindow]
+effect: destructive
+---
+## Steps
+1. `farm.health` — version, `build.pending` (a build on disk not loaded), live Console sessions.
+2. Restart only on the user's request (or after installing a build they asked for):
+```json
+{ "op": "farm.restartExtensionHost", "args": { "resume": true } }
+```
+   When `build.pending` is true it plans a window reload instead (the only thing that loads a new build) and says so. Other agents' running turns are waited for, not cut.
+3. After it comes back: `farm.health` again and compare `bootId` and version.
+
+## Pitfalls
+A window reload closes integrated terminals; open terminals block it. There is a 20-second cooldown.
diff --git a/manual/playbooks/retry-and-rounds.md b/manual/playbooks/retry-and-rounds.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8f5863e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/manual/playbooks/retry-and-rounds.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+id: retry-and-rounds
+title: Make a run robust (retries, smart continue, multi-turn rounds, resume)
+area: tasks
+summary: Retry policies for limits and failures, continuing interrupted work, several turns in one conversation, and continuing an earlier session.
+intents:
+  - if it hits the limit try again after the reset
+  - retry three times if it fails
+  - continue where it stopped
+  - send a follow-up prompt after it answers
+  - continue that earlier session as a task
+keywords: [retry, limit, reset, fail, again, continue, rounds, follow-up, resume, robust]
+ops: [tasks.add, tasks.get, tasks.rerun, tasks.supervision]
+---
+## Options on tasks.add (also on schedules, triggers and templates)
+- `retry`: `{max, delayMin, when, resetAware, behavior}`. `when=limit` (default) retries only when a usage limit stopped the run; `when=any` on any failure. `resetAware=true` waits for the window to reopen. `behavior=smart` continues a run that had already done work instead of starting over; `behavior=supervisor` lets a cheap read-only supervisor decide. Omit `retry` to inherit the user's default; `{max: 0}` means never.
+- `rounds`: further prompts sent into the same conversation once the previous one is answered, optionally after `delayMin`: `[{prompt, delayMin}]`. Headless only.
+- `resume=<sessionId>`: continue an existing conversation instead of starting a new one (its own engine and folder are used).
+```json
+{ "op": "tasks.add", "args": { "agent": "<agent>", "title": "<title>", "prompt": "<round 1>", "retry": { "max": 3, "delayMin": 10, "when": "limit", "resetAware": true, "behavior": "smart" }, "rounds": [ { "prompt": "<round 2: review your result and fix issues>", "delayMin": 0 } ] } }
+```
+## After the fact
+- `tasks.get id=<id>` shows `retryPolicy`, events and retries. Same work again, maybe on another engine: `tasks.rerun id=<id> engine=codex`.
+- A supervised retry row waiting on a decision: `tasks.supervision id=<id> act=run|deterministic|edit`.
diff --git a/manual/playbooks/run-task.md b/manual/playbooks/run-task.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..539497b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/manual/playbooks/run-task.md
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+---
+id: run-task
+title: Have an agent do a job now (task)
+area: tasks
+summary: Queue and start one unattended agent run, follow it, and report the result.
+intents:
+  - have the researcher look into X
+  - run this job with agent Y
+  - ask agent Z to do this in the background
+  - start a task for the writer
+  - do this with codex
+keywords: [task, job, run, background, headless, delegate, now]
+ops: [agents.list, engines.status, engines.models, tasks.add, tasks.get, tasks.list, tasks.cancel]
+effect: run
+---
+## Ask only if missing
+Which agent (offer names from `agents.list`) and what result the user expects. Engine, model and permissions default to the agent's own settings; set them only when the user named them.
+
+## Steps
+1. `agents.list` — confirm the agent exists (use its exact name).
+2. If the user named an engine or model: `engines.status id=<engine>` and `engines.models id=<engine>`.
+3. Write the prompt so the run succeeds alone: goal, inputs (paths, links), constraints, the expected output and where to write it, and "commit when done" for code. Put it in a file:
+```json
+{ "op": "tasks.add", "args": { "agent": "<agent>", "title": "<short title>", "prompt": "<full instructions>", "engine": "claude", "permissionMode": "acceptEdits" } }
+```
+   Call: `tasks.add @task.json`. The answer has the task `id` (and `retryPolicy`).
+4. Follow when needed: `tasks.get id=<id>` (status, `events`, cost, output tail). A parked task shows `heldBy` (concurrency, budget, quota or a retry wait); it starts by itself when that clears.
+
+## Verify and tell the user
+State the task title, agent, engine and id, and where to watch it (Tasks page). When it ends, summarise its output from `tasks.get`; if the user is not in this chat, use playbook notify-user.
+
+## Pitfalls
+- A task costs quota: only when the user asked for this work. For open-ended or large work prefer playbook team-mission (it has limits).
+- Unattended runs cannot answer permission prompts. On Claude choose `permissionMode` up front (`acceptEdits` for edits in its folder, `bypassPermissions` only if the user accepts it); Codex and Gemini tasks are effectively read-only unless widened.
+- `effort` is refused on gemini and opencode.
+- Never repeat `tasks.add` after a timeout with `detail.state: "running"`.
